.TH TRIE 1 "2026-07-31" "bashified" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
trie \- put the morning's accounts in the order a missed message costs most
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trie
[\fB\-\-markdown\fR]
.br
.B trie
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B trie
reads the hand\-filled account inventory and prints the accounts in the order
they are opened, worst consequence first. The decision "where do I even start"
is made once, in the inventory, rather than every morning.
.PP
The inventory is
.IR ACCOUNTS.md ,
a markdown table beside the program, with one row per account and the columns
.IR address ,
.IR provider ,
.IR purpose ,
.IR volume ,
.I stakes
and
.IR pain .
.B trie
reads the first table whose rows carry an address. A row is ranked by its
.I stakes
cell, which holds
.BR high ,
.B medium
or
.BR low ;
ties break on the address, so two runs over an unchanged inventory print the
same order.
.PP
A row with an address but no
.I stakes
value is unranked. It is printed after the ordered rows, marked as unranked,
because dropping it silently reports a smaller inventory than the one on disk.
.PP
.B trie
holds no credential, opens no mailbox, and reaches no network. Its whole domain
is one tracked file in the repository, so what it prints is checkable by reading
that file.
.PP
An inventory in which no row carries a
.I stakes
value produces no order. That case exits
.B 6
with the count of rows seen, never an empty list: an empty list reads as "there
is nothing to open", which is a different fact from "the ranking column is
blank".
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-markdown
Print the order as a markdown document with a heading, for pasting into a note,
instead of the plain\-text listing.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the ordered rows as machine\-readable output.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress commentary; results only.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage summary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version line.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The order was printed.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: an unknown flag, or an argument where none belongs.
.TP
.B 6
BLIND.
.I ACCOUNTS.md
is unreadable, or it holds no row carrying a
.I stakes
value. Reported as an inability to see the ranking, with the number of rows
that do carry an address, never as an empty order.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the version:
.PP
.nf
$ trie --version
trie (bashified)
.fi
.PP
An inventory whose ranking column is blank refuses to invent an order:
.PP
.nf
$ trie
trie: BLIND: no account in ACCOUNTS.md carries a stakes value (1 row with an address, 0 ranked)
trie: this is "I cannot see", NOT "nothing to report".
$ echo $?
6
.fi
.SH "THE COST BOUNDARY"
.B trie
does not spend money. It reads one file in this repository and writes to
standard output. It carries no \fB\-\-summon\fR flag, so
.B \-\-help
alone answers the question of what a run can cost.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ACCOUNTS.md
The account inventory, beside the program, filled by hand and tracked in the
repository. It is the only input.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sort (1),
.BR awk (1),
.BR column (1)
.PP
.B trie
is
.BR sort (1)
over a table a person maintains: the ordering key is a column somebody filled
in, and the output is the same order every time that column is.
